# .github/allowed_signers — the keys whose signature on a v* tag starts a
# release (external audit SUP-024). release.yml's `verify-tag` job checks
# the pushed tag against this file, as committed at the tagged commit, with
# .github/scripts/verify-release-tag.sh (`git verify-tag` with
# gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile); `package` and every later job wait for it.
#
# NO KEY IS LISTED YET. Until the maintainer adds the public half of a
# tag-signing key below, verify-tag refuses every tag: nothing is built,
# signed, attested or published from a tag (fail closed). Adding the key is
# the maintainer's step; nobody else's key belongs here.
#
# Format: ssh-keygen(1) "ALLOWED SIGNERS", one key per line:
#   <principal> namespaces="git" <key type> <base64 public key>
# for instance (the shape only — not a key):
#   maintainer@example.org namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...
# - principal: the maintainer's e-mail (informational; the key decides);
# - namespaces="git" limits the key to git signatures, as xtask's
#   supply_chain tests require;
# - a hardware-backed key (sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com) is welcome;
# - rotation: add the new key, and give the old one
#   valid-before="YYYYMMDD" (namespaces="git",valid-before="20271231")
#   rather than deleting it, so older tags still verify; a compromised key
#   is deleted.
# Signing and checking a tag: CONTRIBUTING.md, "Rilasci".
